The `parseTiffInfo` function takes two arguments: the path to a TIFF image file and a pointer to a `TIFFImageInfo` structure. It parses the given TIFF file and fills in the relevant information about its format in the `TIFFImageInfo` structure.

The function first opens the file using `fopen`, and then checks if it was successfully opened by checking if the returned value is not NULL. If opening the file failed, it sets the status to `FILE_ERROR` and jumps to the end of the function without performing any further operations.

If the file was successfully opened, the function reads the bits per sample and samples per pixel values from the TIFF file using `TIFFBitsPerSample` and `TIFFSamplesPerPixel`, respectively. It then checks if the file is in RGB format (i.e., it has 3 color channels) by calling `TIFFIsRGB`. If it is not, it sets the number of extra samples to 0 and sets the extra samples pointer to NULL.

If the file is in RGB format, the function reads the values of the `EXTRASAMPLES_PERPIXELCOUNT` and `EXTRASAMPLES_FORMATTING` tags from the TIFF file using `TIFFGetField`. It then checks if both of these tags were successfully read, and if so, it sets the number of extra samples to the sum of the values in `sampleCount[0]`, `sampleCount[1]`, and `sampleCount[2]`. If the number of extra samples is greater than 0, it allocates memory for the extra samples using `malloc` and assigns the pointer to the newly allocated memory. If memory allocation fails, it sets the status to `FAILURE`.

Finally, the function closes the file using `fclose`, regardless of whether there was an error or not. If there was an error, it returns the appropriate status code, otherwise it returns the status set in the previous block.']",0,[0],[],True,0,0,0,

